ready for either possibility. Howler made a circle on the table, sniffing for treats. Jackie

25
00:02:49.071 --> 00:02:55.568
caught sight of Grandma watching them from the entrance. Jackie held a treat above Howler's

26
00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:58.906
nose. "Stay." Howler froze, his nose pointed at the treat. Jackie ran her free hand

27
00:02:58.907 --> 00:03:04.132
over the dog's back and then up and down his legs. Howler obediently stayed still.

28
00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:09.217
"Good boy." Jackie fed him the treat. Howler surged forward, looking for another treat. Jackie

29
00:03:09.218 --> 00:03:18.791
placed him back in the middle of the table. "Stay." He froze and she gave

30
00:03:18.792 --> 00:03:24.956
him the treat. Jackie adjusted the dog's feet, remembering what she'd learned about the breed

31
00:03:24.957 --> 00:03:30.446
standard for beagles. Howler's legs were supposed to be straight, and his feet were supposed

32
00:03:30.447 --> 00:03:38.952
to point ahead. Putting one hand under the dog's chin and the other at the tip

33
00:03:38.953 --> 00:03:45.620
of his tail, Jackie looked toward Grandma. "What do you think?" "Nice job." Grandma nodded.

34
00:03:45.621 --> 00:03:52.104
"You did a good job of keeping him focused." Jackie beamed. Maybe working with a

35
00:03:52.105 --> 00:03:57.428
new dog was going to be easier than she had thought. "Showing him will be

36
00:03:57.429 --> 00:04:04.094
harder—there are other dogs and people around." "True," Grandma said. "And Howler doesn't have the

37
00:04:04.095 --> 00:04:08.915
age or experience of Poke. You'll have to be patient with him. But you're off

38
00:04:08.916 --> 00:04:13.396
to a wonderful start." Jackie glanced toward the gate as she patted Howler. Poke lay

39
00:04:13.397 --> 00:04:17.998
quietly watching her, his nose on his front paws. "Should I check Howler's gait next?"

40
00:04:17.999 --> 00:04:26.994
Jackie asked Grandma. "I don't want to do too much on our first day. He'll get

41
00:04:26.995 --> 00:04:32.986
tired and frustrated." "Go ahead and take him around a couple of times," Grandma said.

42
00:04:32.987 --> 00:04:43.579
"Then take a break." Jackie led Howler to the side of the ring. With Poke,

43
00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:47.567
she knew just how fast to run to keep him at his best gait. She

44
00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:51.810
wasn't sure about her speed yet with Howler. This part of training Howler was probably

45
00:04:51.811 --> 00:04:59.984
going to take some time. Holding the leash in her left hand, Jackie led Howler

46
00:04:59.985 --> 00:05:05.642
counterclockwise around the ring. It felt strange to walk with a smaller dog—Poke was taller

47
00:05:05.643 --> 00:05:15.549
than Howler. Jackie quickly increased her speed, and Howler moved into a trot. Then she

48
00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:22.463
saw Grandma watching them with a frown. "Speed up a little," Grandma said. Jackie sped

49
00:05:22.464 --> 00:05:28.366
up. "Slow down," Grandma said. A minute later she walked into the ring. "He's pacing, not

50
00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:31.675
trotting." Jackie groaned. The secret to properly gaiting a dog was making it trot—that meant

51
00:05:31.676 --> 00:05:43.850
it had to move its diagonal feet together, at the same time. When a dog

52
00:05:43.851 --> 00:05:49.181
was pacing, it was moving its feet on the same side together. Trotting made dogs

53
00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:56.074
move in a straight smooth line, which is what handlers wanted while showing their dogs.

54
00:05:56.075 --> 00:06:05.499
"Let me try." Grandma took the leash and circled the ring. Howler trotted comfortably at

55
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:10.330
her side. "He looks good with you. Let me try again," Jackie said. "Go!" Grandma

56
00:06:10.331 --> 00:06:21.072
commanded as Jackie took off, running around the ring. Jackie tried to keep her speed

57
00:06:21.073 --> 00:06:24.064
about the same as Grandma's had been. Then she looked down at Howler's legs. The

58
00:06:24.065 --> 00:06:28.056
two legs on the dog's right side were moving forward together. "Ugh!" She groaned. "He's pacing

59
00:06:28.057 --> 00:06:33.887
again!" Jackie halted. "What am I doing wrong?" Grandma shook her head, a puzzled look

60
00:06:33.888 --> 00:06:38.877
on her face. "I'm not sure. Why don't you quit for now, and I'll think

61
00:06:38.878 --> 00:06:48.219
about it." Jackie didn't like to quit training when things were going poorly. She knew

62
00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:55.281
it was better to end a session on a positive note. But she also knew

63
00:06:55.282 --> 00:07:01.877
that Grandma's suggestion was a good one. She didn't want to teach Howler bad habits.

64
00:07:01.878 --> 00:07:05.202
Plus, she could feel herself getting frustrated. "We'll get it figured out," Grandma assured her.

65
00:07:05.203 --> 00:07:09.194
Jackie praised Howler for the good work he had done and let him into the

66
00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:14.684
exercise yard. Howler took off like a kid let out of school early. Jackie stared

67
00:07:14.685 --> 00:07:20.533
after him, trying to shake off her worry. They had worked well together on the stacking.

68
00:07:20.534 --> 00:07:27.531
Howler knew what to do and he did it—most of the time. But gaiting was

69
00:07:27.532 --> 00:07:32.193
important during shows. Dogs looked stronger, and their backs were straighter when they were trotting,

70
00:07:32.194 --> 00:07:43.506
and it was the handler's job to ensure that the dog looked good. "What if

71
00:07:43.507 --> 00:07:53.198
I can't get it right?" Jackie thought. "I'll be stuck in the novice class forever!"

